WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.120 --> 00:00:05.470
These are satellite images from the deserts
of western China.

00:00:05.470 --> 00:00:09.240
Look closely, and you'll see these huge complexes
being built.

00:00:11.360 --> 00:00:16.200
From the sky, they sort of look like factories
or even schools.

00:00:16.200 --> 00:00:21.460
But look even closer: this line is one facility’s
perimeter wall.

00:00:21.460 --> 00:00:22.740
And these shadows?

00:00:22.750 --> 00:00:26.940
They’re cast by the watchtowers along the
wall.

00:00:26.940 --> 00:00:29.740
This compound isn’t a school or a factory.

00:00:30.920 --> 00:00:33.640
It’s an internment camp.

00:00:33.640 --> 00:00:39.010
Inside these camps, the Chinese government
is detaining as many as 1 million Uighurs,

00:00:39.010 --> 00:00:42.559
China’s mostly Muslim minority.

00:00:42.560 --> 00:00:45.640
China doesn't want the world to know any of
this.

00:00:45.640 --> 00:00:51.180
But the story of these camps is also the story
of how we know about them - and China's efforts

00:00:51.190 --> 00:00:52.370
to cover them up.

00:00:52.370 --> 00:00:56.774
As soon as we began to document the re-education
centers, there was

00:00:56.780 --> 00:00:59.300
Chinese government officials deleting what
we were finding.

00:01:03.400 --> 00:01:08.660
Uighurs mainly live here, in the Xinjiang
province of northwestern China.

00:01:08.660 --> 00:01:13.640
That puts them closer to the capitals of Kyrgyzstan
and Uzbekistan than to Beijing.

00:01:14.800 --> 00:01:19.440
And Uighurs are also closer culturally to
those Turkic groups than they are to the Han

00:01:19.450 --> 00:01:22.020
Chinese, China’s ethnic majority.

00:01:22.020 --> 00:01:25.780
The Uighurs speak a Turkic language. Their
culture is different.

00:01:25.780 --> 00:01:29.710
They have particular styles of music, a whole a whole rich history

00:01:29.710 --> 00:01:31.140
that is unique to them.

00:01:31.140 --> 00:01:33.640
This is Sigal Samuel, a reporter at Vox.

00:01:33.640 --> 00:01:38.650
I've been reporting on the Uighur crisis in
China for about a year now.

00:01:38.650 --> 00:01:45.200
China has been concerned for decades
about the possibility of Uighur separatism.

00:01:45.200 --> 00:01:50.680
Uighurs have actually had their own independent
nation, two separate times in the last century.

00:01:50.680 --> 00:01:57.070
In 1933, they established the Islamic Republic
of East Turkistan here in Kashgar.

00:01:57.070 --> 00:02:01.460
But it crumbled less than a year later when
it was taken over by Chinese forces.

00:02:02.040 --> 00:02:08.780
Then, in 1944, the Soviet Union backed the
creation of the East Turkestan Republic, based here.

00:02:08.940 --> 00:02:14.540
But when China became Communist in 1949, the
Soviet Union turned on East Turkestan, and

00:02:14.540 --> 00:02:16.400
helped China take it over again.

00:02:18.560 --> 00:02:23.800
Part of why the Xinjiang region is so important
to China is that it’s rich with energy resources.

00:02:23.800 --> 00:02:27.870
And as China’s economy grew, so did its
need for energy.

00:02:27.870 --> 00:02:32.629
Today Xinjiang accounts for nearly 40% of
China’s coal reserves.

00:02:32.629 --> 00:02:35.780
And over 20% of the country’s oil and gas.

00:02:35.780 --> 00:02:39.439
It also accounts for 20% of China’s potential
for wind energy.

00:02:39.439 --> 00:02:42.730
China needs resources, it needs
energy.

00:02:42.730 --> 00:02:48.080
It needs the geographical location, the area
on which Xinjiang sits.

00:02:48.100 --> 00:02:49.820
That's where Uighurs are.

00:02:49.829 --> 00:02:50.829
That's where they're living.

00:02:50.829 --> 00:02:55.860
And so China really wants to have a solid
sense of control over that area.

00:02:58.609 --> 00:03:04.069
As far back as the 1950s, China saw an opportunity
to dilute the influence of the potentially

00:03:04.069 --> 00:03:09.780
rebellious Uighurs, and started encouraging
the migration of Han Chinese, into Xinjiang.

00:03:09.780 --> 00:03:10.939
And it worked.

00:03:10.939 --> 00:03:17.530
In 1945, Uighurs made up over 80% of the population, compared to just 6% Han Chinese.

00:03:17.530 --> 00:03:23.360
By 2008, Xinjiang was 46% Uighur compared
to 39% Han Chinese.

00:03:24.040 --> 00:03:28.370
But over the years, as Xinjiang developed
economically, Uighurs were left behind,

00:03:28.370 --> 00:03:34.160
working mostly low-wage jobs in agriculture
while the Han held higher-paying jobs.

00:03:35.400 --> 00:03:40.720
Finally, in 2009, a Uighur protest against
discrimination at the hands of the Han and

00:03:40.739 --> 00:03:43.909
the Chinese government erupted in violence.

00:03:43.909 --> 00:03:50.249
“Bloody riots broke out, pitting ethnic
Uighur Muslims against the dominant Han Chinese.”

00:03:50.249 --> 00:03:53.880
One of the worst riots took place in the provincial
capital of Urumqi.

00:03:53.880 --> 00:03:58.059
About 200 people were killed and hundreds
injured during the unrest.

00:03:58.059 --> 00:04:00.239
That was sort of an inflection point.

00:04:00.239 --> 00:04:05.569
After that, the Chinese really started to crack
down harder on the Uighurs.

00:04:05.569 --> 00:04:09.810
And by 2013, Xinjiang had become even more
important to China.

00:04:09.810 --> 00:04:13.879
The country launched the “Belt and Road”
initiative, a trillion-dollar investment in

00:04:13.879 --> 00:04:19.440
things like fiber optic cables, train lines,
and gas pipelines meant to boost the country’s

00:04:19.440 --> 00:04:25.120
economic and political influence around the
world by making it easier to trade with China.

00:04:25.120 --> 00:04:29.660
If you plot these projects on a map you’ll
see a lot of them pass through Xinjiang, making

00:04:29.660 --> 00:04:33.900
the province arguably the most important corridor
for the whole project.

00:04:33.900 --> 00:04:38.320
China would need to ensure that Xinjiang remained
securely in its hands.

00:04:38.320 --> 00:04:42.920
The Uighurs came to be perceived
and painted more as a threat,

00:04:42.920 --> 00:04:45.820
as a separatist threat, as an extremist threat.

00:04:45.820 --> 00:04:52.080
In 2016 and 2017, the country enacted a series
of “de-extremification” policies aimed

00:04:52.080 --> 00:04:55.200
at Muslims, like banning long beards.

00:04:55.200 --> 00:04:58.919
And Xinjiang was effectively turned into a
hi-tech police state.

00:04:58.919 --> 00:05:03.020
So this kind of thing is happening
all over the country, but in Xinjiang it's

00:05:03.020 --> 00:05:05.860
been just increased by orders of magnitude.

00:05:05.860 --> 00:05:10.820
We're talking about Uighurs
having to hand over their phones at checkpoints.

00:05:10.830 --> 00:05:16.050
We're even talking about QR codes being
installed on the outside of their homes.

00:05:16.050 --> 00:05:20.060
But the most brutal part of this crackdown
was hidden to the world at first.

00:05:23.880 --> 00:05:27.140
In this image you see the opening of this
facility.

00:05:27.140 --> 00:05:31.040
The signage, it says "De-extremification reeducation center."

00:05:32.200 --> 00:05:36.900
Around 2017, China started building
these internment camps,

00:05:36.900 --> 00:05:42.550
these large scale places to detain Uighurs.
China says that these camps are

00:05:42.550 --> 00:05:47.360
necessary because the Uighurs are a terrorist threat.

00:05:47.360 --> 00:05:48.680
A separatist threat.

00:05:48.680 --> 00:05:51.810
People who are infected with extremist thinking.

00:05:51.810 --> 00:05:55.770
But it wasn’t until Uighurs who had been
detained told their stories, that the picture

00:05:55.770 --> 00:05:59.280
from inside the camps came into clearer focus.

00:05:59.280 --> 00:06:04.710
They're forced to memorize and
recite Communist Party propaganda every day.

00:06:04.710 --> 00:06:10.180
They're often forced to criticize their own
Islamic beliefs and to criticize the beliefs

00:06:10.180 --> 00:06:11.669
of their fellow detainees.

00:06:11.669 --> 00:06:14.810
“We had to sing songs hailing the Communist
Party.

00:06:14.810 --> 00:06:18.360
We had to repeat in Chinese, ‘long live
[Chinese president] Xi Jinping!

00:06:18.360 --> 00:06:21.540
There have been reports of death,
of torture.

00:06:21.540 --> 00:06:26.220
“Three guards surrounded me and abused
me.

00:06:29.540 --> 00:06:33.460
"Each time I was electrocuted, my whole body would shake."

00:06:33.659 --> 00:06:40.100
So there's this atmosphere of
just trying to uproot what you believe in.

00:06:42.930 --> 00:06:46.000
At first, China denied the existence of 
these camps...

00:07:00.580 --> 00:07:02.849
But activists and academics fought back.

00:07:02.849 --> 00:07:05.540
A lot of people around the world

00:07:05.540 --> 00:07:08.000
are scouring the Internet for evidence

00:07:08.000 --> 00:07:09.990
of China's internment camps for Uighurs.

00:07:09.990 --> 00:07:15.969
In terms of the strategies and tools
that I've used and others have used to uncover

00:07:15.969 --> 00:07:23.020
evidence of these camps, it's quite simply
a computer and knowledge of Chinese and thinking

00:07:23.020 --> 00:07:26.849
about what ways whats words, especially government
websites, would use.

00:07:26.849 --> 00:07:29.990
People have unearthed government
documents...

00:07:29.990 --> 00:07:34.900
"And then we had growing visual evidence. We're looking at satellite images."

00:07:35.020 --> 00:07:40.700
We could actually trace the creation and expansion of the reeducation camp.

00:07:41.580 --> 00:07:45.200
It was a matter of, I think luck or chance

00:07:45.200 --> 00:07:45.940
I uncovered this image.

00:07:45.940 --> 00:07:51.520
And until then, we didn't have
that piece of visual evidence that said this

00:07:51.530 --> 00:07:52.550
is what it is.

00:07:52.550 --> 00:07:55.330
And this is what the Chinese government's
calling it.

00:07:55.330 --> 00:07:56.740
Tim isn’t alone.

00:07:56.740 --> 00:08:01.230
There’s a whole network of “web sleuths”
around the world using basic internet tools

00:08:01.230 --> 00:08:02.669
to document what China doesn’t

00:08:02.669 --> 00:08:03.939
want the world to see.

00:08:03.939 --> 00:08:07.400
And they’ve gotten China to change their
story, at least a little.

00:08:07.400 --> 00:08:12.389
China was denying that these re education centers exist, until journalists

00:08:12.389 --> 00:08:17.129
and academics and others started to really
amass a body of evidence that was so convincing

00:08:17.129 --> 00:08:19.560
that China couldn't just deny it anymore.

00:08:20.220 --> 00:08:24.480
China took a different approach and started
admitting that these facilities exist, but

00:08:24.500 --> 00:08:28.920
carefully painted them as training schools
for potential criminals or terrorists.

00:08:38.430 --> 00:08:42.310
In the meantime, the camps are still there
and growing.

00:08:42.310 --> 00:08:47.690
This camp, one of China’s largest, was as
big as the nearby city of Dabancheng in 2017.

00:08:47.690 --> 00:08:52.880
But by 2018, the camp had expanded to twice the
size.

00:08:53.580 --> 00:08:58.440
From China's perspective they think it's worth
it. They want to make sure Xinjiang is an

00:08:58.440 --> 00:09:01.620
area of the country that they have total control
over.

00:09:01.620 --> 00:09:06.860
And if that comes with a high human cost and
even a reputational blow on the international

00:09:06.900 --> 00:09:11.400
stage, China so far seems willing to do that
anyway.

